<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#frankokratia">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frankokratia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1204">1204</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Latinokratia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frankokratia ('rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>') is the period of Western, chiefly French and Italian, rule in Greek lands after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> of 1204. It covered the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, Venetian colonies and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> states; most were lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> by the fifteenth century, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> held some islands much longer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frankokratia.jpg" alt="Frankokratia" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LatinEmpire · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LatinEmpire2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Frankokratia, also called Latinokratia, and for the Venetian domains the Venetian Occupation, names the collection of primarily French and Italian states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> and colonies established on the territory of the partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> after the Sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>. The terms come from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, the name the Orthodox Greeks gave to Western French and Italians whose homelands had once belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish Empire</a>. The period's span differs by region, because the situation was volatile: Frankish states fragmented and changed hands, and Greek successor states reconquered many areas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-empire">Latin Empire</a> (1204 to 1261), centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>, claimed nominal suzerainty over the other crusader states. It shrank to little more than its capital, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a> captured in 1261. Among the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> states, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> (1205 to 1224) fell to the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, and the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> (1205 to 1432) in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> became the strongest, until the Byzantine Despotate of the Morea conquered it. The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> (1205 to 1458) passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalan Company</a> in 1311 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acciaiuoli-family">Acciaiuoli family</a> in 1388. The Duchy of the Archipelago, founded by the Sanudo family in 1207, lasted until 1579.</p><p>Genoese families such as the Gattilusi held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> and other north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> islands, and the Maona company held Chios until 1566. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> built the most durable holdings: the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Candia</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> (1211 to 1715), Corfu for most of the period from 1386 to 1797, the coastal fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a>, held until 1500, and the whole island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Negroponte</a> from 1390 to 1470. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> from 1310 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ousted them in 1522. Many areas were captured by the Ottomans between the 14th and 17th centuries, and the Ionian Islands and some islands and forts stayed Venetian until the turn of the 19th century, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> itself was restored in 1261.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> left a mark on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a>. The fragmentation of the Byzantine world caused communication problems and divided the common language into dialects, and since the Franks used the speech of ordinary people for administration, the prestige of the literary language declined. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> loanwords, mostly Italian and including Venetian, then French, entered Greek. The vocabulary of feudal law and property was mainly French, while that of trade and shipping was Italian. Frankish place names are the most significant influence, and a number of Greek given names are of Frankish origin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frankokratia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name &quot;Latin Empire&quot; was given by historians in the 16th century; its own documents called it the imperium Constantinopolitanum, and the Byzantines spoke of the <b>Frankokratia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the mid-thirteenth century a mint at Clarentza struck deniers tournois on the French model, which circulated throughout <b>Latin Greece</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
